On Wed, Apr 13, 2016 at 10:02 PM, albernererhelge <eckene...@web.de> wrote:
> Thank you both for the quick response. Glad to say it worked quite well so far!
>
> Short question: we separated both analyses, "fixed-nodes" and "including direct ancestors as hooks", with slightly different results. Is it useful to implement both within one analysis? You wrote that the fossils geographic range will automatocally be forced at the node below, so I guess both analyses lead to the same result, only with a higher likelihood in "including direct ancestors as hooks"?
>
> Regards

There is a significant difference between the two strategies:

1. If you have fossils as "hook" tips, then the fossils are data, and the likelihood of those data is added to the likelihood of the other tip data in the tree

2. If you have fossils as fixnode constraints at nodes, then the fossils aren't data, they are just additional limits on the model.

So, the LnLs between the two analyses are not comparable, because likelihoods are only comparable when two different models are being fit to *identical* data. (Likelihood = "probability of the data, given a model")

There are some other differences -- hooknodes are essentially forcing a particular range somewhere in the middle of a branch, with a special "hooknode" where there is no cladogenesis process;  whereas fixnodes are fixing it at a node where there is standard cladogenesis processes allowed.

IMHO using fossils as tips and/or hooktips is a truer representation of the data, and fixing the ranges at nodes is more for hypothesis testing or measuring the affect on the likelihood of different hypotheses about what the ancestral states were.  Both could be useful, and they might give similar results especially if the "hooknodes" are just below where the fixnodes are, but they aren't identical.

Yes, they can be combined, if you have a reason to do so.

Cheers!
Nick

